{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["And the reason of <b>And I will walk among you</b> is that My conduct toward you will be publicly manifest, like a king walking in the midst of his camp, providing all their needs. This is the way of the words of the covenant according to their plain sense, and it is true, and so He will indeed do for them. Now Scripture did not mention here the reward of the survival of the souls in the World of Souls and in the World to Come after the resurrection of the dead, because their survival is obligated by the way of formation, as I explained in the matter of כרת (Ramban on Leviticus 18:29), and the punishment is that He cuts off the guilty from among them, while all the rest survive in the way of their formation.\n\nBut by way of truth, these blessings are still blessings of the heaven among the supernal ones, as I explained: \"And I will give peace in the land,\" \"And My soul shall not abhor you,\" and likewise \"And I will walk among you\" — a hint to the attribute which our Rabbis called \"Shekhinah,\" from \"And I will set My Mishkhan in your midst,\" and they say (Menahot 86a) that the Shekhinah rests upon Israel, and they said (Genesis Rabbah 19:7) the essence of the Shekhinah was among the lower ones. And behold, here Garden of Eden and the World to Come are alluded to for those who know Him. And these blessings in their fullness will not be except when all Israel do the will of their Father, and the structure of heaven and earth is complete upon its foundation, and there are in the Torah no complete blessings like these, which are the words of the covenant and the conditions between HaKadosh Barukh Hu and us.\n\nAnd know that Israel never attained these blessings in their fullness — not the many and not the individuals among them — for their merit did not reach that, as they said (Mo'ed Qatan 16a) concerning David, \"and he aroused his spear against eight hundred casualties,\" and he would grieve over the two hundred — a bat kol went forth and said to him: only in the matter of Uriah the Hittite (II Kings 15:5). And therefore you will find that our Rabbis of blessed memory expound these verses as referring to the time to come: \"This teaches that an infant of Israel is destined to be stretched out...\" (Torat Kohanim, chapter 2:2); \"HaKadosh Barukh Hu is destined to stroll with the righteous in the World to Come\" (ibid., chapter 3:3). For it was not fulfilled, but it will be fulfilled with us in the time of perfection."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "In the Talmudic passage cited from Mo'ed Qatan 16a, the phrase about David's grief over the two hundred does not appear in our standard text of that page; the reference may be to a parallel tradition. The citation (II Kings 15:5) is Ramban's own and refers to the verse about David's affliction; the standard chapter-verse reference for the David narrative is II Samuel, but Ramban cites it as מלכים."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:01:01.521150406Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}